Stuttering
Paediatric & Neonatal
Sound/syllable repetitions, Prolongations/blocks, Secondary physical tension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-048
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe deterioration in speech fluency accompanied by extreme physical distress and complete mutism, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute neurogenic injury or seizure activity.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a child who suddenly developed severe physical facial twitching while attempting to speak, followed by complete refusal to talk. Immediate assessment was required to definitively exclude acute stroke, complex partial seizures, or a severe acute dystonic reaction prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He was trying to tell a story, his jaw started jerking violently, he couldn't get a single word out, and now he won't speak at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents following an acute exacerbation of stuttering, characterized by sudden, profound blocking and word prolongation that completely disrupted speech fluency tonight. The caregivers report frequent repetitions of sounds and syllables over the last year, but tonight the dysfluency involved words produced with an excess of severe physical tension. There were accompanying physical motor tics, specifically eye blinking and head jerking, strictly during the vocal blocks. The extreme physical struggle resulted in profound anxiety about speaking, leading directly to the current state of active avoidance of social communication (mutism). Exacerbation of the dysfluency occurred strictly under psychological stress while the child was trying to quickly explain a conflict with a sibling.
Functional Status: Alert, physically normal, completely selectively mute due to severe embarrassment and fear.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute psychological distress for the child; caregiver panic regarding a suspected neurological event.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Alert, making normal eye contact, nodding appropriately, appears physically tense but in no acute medical distress.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is globally intact. Cranial nerves II-XII are entirely normal with symmetric facial movement and tongue protrusion, completely ruling out acute bulbar palsy or stroke. Muscle tone, power, and coordination are normal. There are absolutely no involuntary choreiform or dystonic movements at rest. When the child is gently encouraged to whisper a single word, mild syllable repetition occurs without the severe facial jerking seen earlier. The diagnostic tetrad of sound/syllable repetitions, prolongations/blocks, and secondary physical tension is explicitly confirmed historically.
Environmental Observations: Parents are hovering closely, repeatedly asking the child to speak.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications. No recent use of anti-emetics that could cause acute dystonia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder (Stuttering) - Status: Acute, severe exacerbation with secondary avoidance
Differentials:

Acute Stroke / Transient Ischemic Attack - Ruled out explicitly by the completely normal cranial nerve examination, absence of facial droop, normal limb strength, and intact receptive language skills.

Complex Partial Seizure - Ruled out by the lack of altered consciousness during the event, precise situational trigger (speaking under pressure), and the child's complete awareness of the struggle.

Acute Dystonic Reaction - Ruled out by the lack of exposure to dopamine antagonists, and the strict confinement of the physical movements exclusively to the act of attempted speech.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk), Moderate (Psychological distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The abrupt presentation of severe facial tension and secondary mutism in a young child necessitated an urgent after-hours neurological assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical need to exclude an acute stroke or seizure disorder manifesting as sudden expressive aphasia or dystonia. Objective clinical evaluation definitively confirmed an intact neurological baseline, explicitly diagnosing a severe behavioral and mechanical exacerbation of a known stutter. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the parents to severe trauma regarding a presumed brain injury and exacerbated the child's acute anxiety and speech avoidance. Immediate intervention provided diagnostic safety, validating the child's severe frustration and providing immediate environmental de-escalation strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided to the parents regarding the absolute absence of a stroke or brain injury. Advised immediate cessation of all demands for the child to speak tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: The parents must actively model extremely slow, relaxed, and paused speech themselves. When the child eventually attempts to speak, parents must provide a highly positive, visibly relaxed facial expression and wait a full 5 seconds before responding. This actively removes the perceived time pressure, positively reinforcing a calm communicative environment and directly reducing the severe physiological tension driving the vocal blocks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for specialized pediatric speech-language pathology.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to monitor referral status and psychological well-being.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the child develops a sudden droop on one side of their face, becomes completely unable to understand simple commands, develops sudden limb weakness, or has a seizure.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Stuttering severity fluctuates dynamically; the physical tics are a learned behavioral reaction to the block, not a neurological spasm.
Clinical Course: The acute mutism will resolve once the child feels the environmental pressure to perform has been completely removed. Early, intensive speech therapy is highly effective in establishing fluent speech patterns.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the child's face droops, if they cannot lift both arms, or if they suddenly cannot understand what you are saying.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sudden jerking was not a seizure or a stroke. It was the child's desperate, forceful physical attempt to push a stuck word out. The vocal cords temporarily locked up due to anxiety and pressure. The child has stopped speaking entirely because the physical effort was terrifying and exhausting.
Actionable Steps: Do not ask the child to speak, repeat words, or "slow down" tonight. Communicate using yes/no questions and physical gestures. Ensure all adults in the household speak very slowly and calmly around the child to naturally lower the communication pace.

